  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Somalia Response 25/26</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project Information:  This project is providing sustainable access to safe and reliable drinking water for 30,000 displaced people and vulnerable host communities across six villages in the Sool and Sanaag regions of Somaliland. It is installing new desalination plants powered by renewable energy, building pipeline networks, and setting up community tap stands to replace unsafe and saline water sources. By using modern desalination technology, the project is transforming the way water is supplied in one of the driest regions in the world, where frequent droughts and degraded groundwater have left families at risk. It is also establishing locally led water management systems and cost -recovery mechanisms so that communities can maintain the supply equitably and sustainably long after the project ends. In doing so, the project is not only responding to the urgent water crisis but also reducing protection risks, improving health outcomes, and creating a model for long -term water solutions that can be replicated across Somaliland and beyond.     For more information, please contact Hassan Siyad (Senior Media &amp; Comms Officer &#x2013; Oxfam in Somalia)</narrative>
  </description>
